The failure means the transformed AST is structurally inconsistent: some node is now reachable as a child of one AST node, but its `Parent()` still points somewhere else.

`EveryChildHasValidParent` does essentially:

```cpp
ast->Iterate([ast](child) {
    assert(child->Parent() == ast);
});
```

`TopLevelStatements` is a heavy mutation phase: it moves top-level statements into synthetic `ETSGLOBAL`, static blocks, namespace-transformed classes, etc. If you use raw vector mutation like:

```cpp
classDef->BodyForUpdate().push_back(node);
program->Ast()->StatementsForUpdates().erase(...);
```

the parent pointer is not updated automatically. Then verifier fails.

Fix it by maintaining parent pointers whenever you move/create/replace nodes:

```cpp
globalClass->EmplaceBody(node);
node->SetParent(globalClass);
```

or prefer helpers that already do it:

```cpp
block->AddStatement(stmt);      // sets stmt parent
module->AddStatements(stmts);   // sets parents
classDef->AddProperties(...);   // sets parents
```

For newly constructed nodes, use:

```cpp
util::NodeAllocator::ForceSetParent<T>(allocator, ...);
```

If the same AST node must remain in the old place too, do not share it: clone it. One node cannot have two valid parents.

If this happens only with AST history enabled, the verifier may also need to compare current history nodes instead of raw pointers:

```cpp
auto *parent = child->Parent();
if (parent == nullptr || parent->GetHistoryNode() != ast->GetHistoryNode()) {
    ...
}
```

So the practical rule: after `TopLevelStatements`, every direct child in every updated container must have `SetParent()` pointing to that exact logical container, or be cloned if reused.

